"""Original HVAC buying chapters with the established HomeCostEngine budget workflow."""
from pathlib import Path
import runpy,copy
base=runpy.run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('renovation-blueprint.py')))
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,deck,paragraphs,example,rows,conclusion,prompts,checks,task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))
def method(index,title):
    c=copy.deepcopy(base['CHAPTERS'][index]);c['title']=title
    replacements={'renovation':'HVAC replacement','Make the kitchen usable for two cooks':'Make the upstairs rooms comfortable','buy new cabinets':'buy a larger air conditioner','A bathroom project in a one-bath home has a different temporary-use problem from the same work in an empty property.':'Replacing the only working system during harsh weather creates different temporary-comfort needs from a planned change during mild weather.','fixture allowance':'equipment-selection allowance','optional finish upgrade':'optional control upgrade'}
    def adapt(s):
        for a,b in replacements.items():s=s.replace(a,b)
        return s
    for k in ['deck','example','conclusion','task']:c[k]=adapt(c[k])
    for k in ['paragraphs','prompts','checks']:c[k]=[adapt(x) for x in c[k]]
    CHAPTERS.append(c)

method(0,'Define the HVAC replacement brief')
chapter('Inventory the existing system','Build a record before requesting a replacement proposal.',[
'List the indoor and outdoor equipment, controls, fuel or electrical supply and the areas the system serves. Record model and serial information from accessible documents or have the service provider supply it. Keep installation invoices, service reports and warranty information with the inventory rather than relying on equipment age remembered from a previous owner.',
'Describe observed problems by room, season and time: uneven temperature, humidity concerns, noise, cycling or recurring service calls. Observations help the contractor investigate; they are not diagnoses. Record changes to windows, insulation, occupancy or floor area that may affect the assessment of current needs.',
'Distinguish equipment that is retained, replaced or awaiting evaluation. An outdoor-unit replacement may involve connected indoor equipment, controls and installation scope that a product-only quote does not describe. Ask who confirms the suitability of every retained component before accepting a partial replacement.',
'Use existing records and qualified service visits rather than opening equipment or testing utilities yourself. This book organizes the buyer side of the decision. It provides no refrigerant, combustion, wiring, repair or commissioning procedure and cannot determine whether an existing system is safe to operate.'
], 'An owner records ten system components: four proposed for retention, four for replacement and two needing evaluation. The two unresolved items remain decision gates, not assumed zero-cost inclusions.', [('Retain','4 items'),('Replace','4 items'),('Evaluate','2 items'),('Total inventory','10 items')],
'Component counts organize the record but do not establish repair scope or system compatibility. Give each evaluation an accountable professional and deadline.',
['Equipment and control inventory / document references','Served areas / observed issues / timing','Past repairs / warranties / property changes','Retain / replace / evaluate status','Professional evaluations / deadlines / affected choices'],
['Observed problems are separated from diagnoses.','Retained components have a verification owner.','No owner equipment opening or testing is required.'], 'Send the inventory and comfort observations to each contractor before asking for a final quote.')

chapter('Compare repair and replacement scenarios','Use documented alternatives without inventing a remaining life.',[
'Ask the service provider to state the diagnosed problem and a defined repair alternative where one is appropriate. Keep the assessment, repair scope and replacement proposal separate. A repair estimate does not promise how long every other component will last, and an equipment-age rule alone does not describe the condition of this system.',
'Compare the immediate cost, service obligations, downtime and unresolved issues for each alternative. Identify what the repair addresses and what it leaves unchanged. If the replacement proposal claims to solve a room comfort issue, ask which assessment and scope support that claim rather than treating a new model number as proof.',
'Use a clearly stated planning horizon for a cost scenario, with future repairs and operating costs labeled as assumptions. Do not present guessed remaining life or future resale value as fact. A cash comparison can explain the decision without pretending to predict equipment failures.',
'Keep safety findings and urgent professional advice outside a simple price ranking. Ask the responsible qualified provider to explain the condition and permitted options. This chapter is a record for reviewing assessed alternatives, not a diagnostic tool or a recommendation to keep operating faulty equipment.'
], 'A fictional assessed repair costs $1,100 now. A separate replacement option costs $9,000. The immediate spending difference is $7,900; neither future repairs nor energy use have been estimated.', [('Defined repair','$1,100'),('Replacement alternative','$9,000'),('Immediate cash difference','$7,900'),('Future lifetime cost','Not established')],
'The lower immediate cost is not automatically the better decision. Record assessed condition, unresolved comfort issues, warranty and realistic future assumptions.',
['Diagnosis / assessor / dated evidence','Defined repair / exclusions / service terms','Replacement scope / claimed outcomes / evidence','Planning horizon / future costs / unknowns','Decision rationale / urgent findings / approvals'],
['Neither option is assigned an invented useful life.','The same desired outcomes are compared.','Professional condition findings remain visible.'], 'Ask for a written explanation of what each assessed option fixes and leaves unresolved.')

chapter('Ask for a property-specific sizing basis','Floor area and the old unit are not a complete design.',[
'Request the basis used to select equipment for the actual home. ENERGY STAR quality-installation guidance emphasizes property characteristics rather than relying only on a rule of thumb [2]. Record who performs the assessment and which current building and comfort information they used.',
'Ask how room needs, building changes and proposed equipment performance are considered together. A report should be understandable enough that the owner can identify the assumptions and the selected option, while technical design remains with the responsible professional. This handbook does not calculate a heating or cooling load.',
'Keep units clear when reading reports. A ton of cooling represents 12,000 BTU per hour of cooling capacity; it is not equipment weight. Nominal capacity labels and performance at specified conditions are different records. Ask the contractor to explain the comparison rather than rounding a house-size shortcut into an order.',
'Record the proposed equipment and the assessment version together. A significant design or building change may require the professional to review the selection again. Do not buy a larger unit simply because the current system struggles in one room; ask what the assessment identifies as the cause.'
], 'A fictional comparison sheet translates a nominal 2.5-ton cooling label into 30,000 BTU/h. This is a unit conversion only; it is not the calculated requirement of any home.', [('One cooling ton','12,000 BTU/h'),('2.5 × 12,000','30,000 BTU/h'),('Property heating or cooling load','Not calculated here'),('Selection responsibility','Qualified designer or contractor')],
'The conversion cannot tell you which equipment to buy. Obtain the actual assessment and proposed-system performance information from the responsible professional.',
['Assessment provider / date / method / version','Property assumptions / building changes / room issues','Calculated needs / professional explanation','Proposed capacities / units / performance conditions','Selection approval / questions / review triggers'],
['The selection has a documented property-specific basis.','Nominal ratings and design needs are distinguished.','No floor-area shortcut is used as this book\'s design.'], 'Ask the contractor to explain the sizing basis and provide the record with the proposal.')

chapter('Compare system configurations on equal scope','A different system can create work outside the equipment line.',[
'Describe each proposed configuration and the areas it serves. A central ducted system, a ductless arrangement, a heat pump or a separate heating and cooling combination can involve different installation boundaries. Ask the contractor which alternatives are suitable for the property and why.',
'Use the same desired outcomes and scope categories for each option: equipment, distribution, utilities, controls, access, approvals and restoration. A lower equipment price can hide different served areas or excluded supporting work. Keep those differences explicit before comparing a complete installed total.',
'Discuss room control, visible equipment, service access, maintenance and how future repairs would be handled. A product brochure cannot establish the experience of the actual installed arrangement. Record the tradeoff that matters to the household rather than ranking options solely by a technical label.',
'Confirm responsibility for existing equipment that is removed or retained. If the proposal changes fuels or utility needs, request a coordinated assessment of the supporting work. This chapter does not establish that a particular configuration fits the property; it helps record the professional reasoning and the cost boundaries.'
], 'Fictional Option A includes $8,800 equipment and installation plus $700 distribution work. Option B is $9,100 installed but excludes $600 electrical work. Other scope is assumed equal only for this example.', [('Option A comparison total','$9,500'),('Option B comparison total','$9,700'),('Difference after listed additions','$200'),('Other scope differences','Still require confirmation')],
'The $200 result is useful only if served areas, installation scope and required outcomes match. It is not a market comparison between system types.',
['Proposed configurations / served areas / suitability reviewer','Equipment / distribution / utility / control scope','Removal / retention / access / restoration','Matched installed totals / exclusions / unknowns','Household priorities / tradeoffs / decision rationale'],
['Served areas and outcomes are matched.','Utility changes have a responsible verifier.','Equipment price is not confused with full project cost.'], 'Ask each bidder to show the complete installed scope for the suitable alternatives.')

chapter('Record the exact matched equipment','A brand name is not a complete specification.',[
'Request model references for all proposed indoor and outdoor units and the connected controls. Record accessories and other specified components that affect the installation. A brand, series or nominal efficiency description is not enough to identify what will actually be supplied.',
'Ask the contractor for the applicable system combination and performance documentation. Keep it with the proposed model list, including assumptions about retained components. Do not assume that independently attractive ratings for two products describe the performance of their installed combination.',
'Record what may be substituted if a component is unavailable, and require the responsible professional to confirm the revised combination before approval. A substitute can affect compatibility, ratings, controls, service terms or schedule. A similar-looking model number does not establish equivalence.',
'Check the final delivery and commissioning records against the approved schedule. Keep model and serial information with warranty and service documents. This worksheet records the selected system and evidence; it does not certify a match or interpret technical product requirements in place of the qualified provider.'
], 'A fictional quote lists $5,200 for an equipment combination, $450 for required listed accessories and $250 for a compatible control. These items are expressly not in the separate installation line.', [('Defined equipment combination','$5,200'),('Listed accessories','$450'),('Compatible control','$250'),('Supply subtotal','$5,900')],
'If a package already includes its controls or accessories, do not add them again. The subtotal excludes installation and other project scope.',
['Indoor / outdoor / control model schedule','Combination and rating evidence / retained components','Required accessories / supply inclusions','Approved substitutions / reviewer / effect on scope','Delivered serial numbers / warranty / completion record'],
['Every supplied major component is identified.','Combination evidence matches the proposed models.','Substitutions need documented professional review.'], 'Attach the complete approved model schedule to the accepted proposal before equipment is ordered.')

chapter('Clarify ducts, distribution and airflow work','New equipment does not define the distribution scope.',[
'List the areas served and record comfort observations by room. Ask the qualified contractor to evaluate the existing distribution and any proposed changes. ENERGY STAR quality-installation guidance includes duct evaluation and appropriate airflow as installation questions [2]. An owner observation is useful input, not a duct diagnosis.',
'Separate inspection or testing from repair, replacement, balancing and restoration where those are different tasks. Request the scope and the record that will demonstrate completion. The phrase ductwork as needed should not be the only explanation of an open-ended allowance.',
'Identify accessible and concealed areas and the work required to reach them. Ask who restores disturbed surfaces and whether any retained duct or room outlet remains outside the proposed scope. A quoted room improvement needs an explanation of which work is expected to address it.',
'Do not specify airflow, duct dimensions or pressure limits from this workbook. Have the responsible professional establish the design and manufacturer requirements for the actual system. Save the evaluation and completion records so future service questions can be connected to the installed arrangement.'
], 'A fictional distribution quote separates $250 assessment, $650 defined repair and $300 agreed balancing work. The installer has confirmed that none of the lines duplicates the equipment installation scope.', [('Assessment','$250'),('Defined repair','$650'),('Agreed balancing scope','$300'),('Distribution subtotal','$1,200')],
'The arithmetic does not establish that the proposed work fixes a particular comfort problem. Confirm the assessed cause, scope and completion evidence.',
['Served rooms / observations / evaluation record','Assessment / testing / repair / balancing boundaries','Access and restoration / retained exclusions','Professional design basis / completion evidence','Defined cost / allowance / approval process'],
['Distribution is evaluated rather than assumed suitable.','Unclear work is not treated as included.','No workbook value is used as an airflow design.'], 'Ask for the distribution findings and a priced response to each material issue before comparing final bids.')

chapter('Coordinate power, fuel and condensate scope','Supporting connections need a named trade and a price.',[
'Ask the responsible professionals to identify the utilities and connections required by the proposed system. Separate retained connections from changed electrical, fuel, venting or condensate work where applicable. The equipment supplier and installing trades must agree on the actual requirements.',
'Record who assesses existing capacity and condition, who performs connected work and who arranges applicable approvals. A standard connection allowance is not evidence that the existing service can support a proposed change. This book provides no instructions for wiring, gas work, refrigerant handling or drainage installation.',
'Include access, disconnection, removal and finish restoration in the scope discussion. A task excluded by the HVAC contractor still needs an accountable party if the project requires it. Confirm the timing of supporting work before committing to the equipment installation date.',
'Keep unresolved utility questions as decision gates with a deadline and verifier. Ask for written changes if the assessed condition differs from the quote assumption. Reconcile each additional task against the existing allowance so the same connection work does not appear in two invoices.'
], 'A fictional project needs $600 separately quoted electrical work and $280 defined condensate work. The HVAC base contract expressly excludes both and includes the remaining standard connections.', [('Electrical scope','$600'),('Condensate scope','$280'),('Known supporting additions','$880'),('Unassessed utility changes','Not included')],
'Only add the $880 if those tasks are absent from the base price. Unknown capacity or condition still requires professional assessment, not a zero budget line.',
['Required connections / actual system specifications','Existing-condition and capacity reviewer','HVAC versus supporting-trade responsibilities','Disconnection / access / restoration / approval scope','Confirmed price / open allowances / timing gates'],
['The proposed equipment and supporting scope match.','Excluded required work has an owner.','No utility modification is authorized by this worksheet.'], 'Obtain the supporting-trade scope before accepting a final installation date.')

chapter('Choose controls for the actual system','A thermostat should fit both equipment and household use.',[
'Describe the control functions the household needs: schedules, room adjustments, remote access or a simple local interface. Record the selected equipment and ask the responsible installer which controls and accessories are compatible. A popular thermostat is not automatically the right choice for every system.',
'Separate the price of the control from wiring changes, sensors, configuration and owner instruction. If the control is already included in a matched equipment package, record it as included rather than buying another one without confirmation. Identify recurring service or subscription costs where applicable.',
'Ask what works locally if an internet connection or online service is unavailable. Record account ownership, handover and how the owner receives operating instructions. Do not share private account credentials in the project worksheet; keep this record focused on responsibility and documentation.',
'Have the installer explain the settings and operating approach for the actual system. This handbook does not prescribe control sequences or override manufacturer instructions. Keep the model reference, configuration record and support contact with the final equipment file.'
], 'A fictional control costs $240, with $110 defined setup work and a separately optional online service at $4 per month. No installation or subscription is assumed free.', [('Control and setup','$350'),('Optional annual service: 12 × $4','$48'),('Optional service over five years','$240'),('Five-year initial plus service','$590')],
'The five-year comparison excludes future repairs and price changes. Confirm whether the online service is necessary for the features the household actually wants.',
['Required functions / equipment compatibility reviewer','Control / sensors / setup / included package items','Online features / offline behavior / recurring costs','Account handover / operating instruction / support','Approved model / professional configuration record'],
['Compatibility has been confirmed for the installed system.','Recurring costs are visible.','No private credentials are recorded in the worksheet.'], 'Ask the installer to demonstrate the household functions before approving an optional control upgrade.')

chapter('Compare efficiency and full ownership cost','A rating is one input, not a guaranteed utility bill.',[
'Keep the proposed rating and its supporting documentation with the actual equipment combination. Ask the contractor what the rating represents and which assumptions matter to your home. Do not turn a product label into a promised percentage reduction in the entire household utility bill.',
'Use property-specific energy estimates when available and label the comparison inputs: usage, climate, utility rates, system scope and maintenance. Compare like functions and comfort assumptions. A change in fuel or use pattern may require a more careful comparison than subtracting two electricity numbers.',
'Simple payback divides an incremental initial cost by an assumed annual net saving. It ignores financing, replacement timing, future rates and other costs unless explicitly included. Show sensitivity to the assumptions rather than presenting one precise result as a prediction.',
'Keep rebates and other incentives separate from the gross project price. Before counting any amount as available funding, verify the current program, selected equipment, installation timing and eligibility with the responsible administrator or adviser. No incentive amount or tax entitlement is assumed in this book.'
], 'A fictional upgrade adds $1,500 to an otherwise matched scope. A separate hypothetical estimate suggests $150 annual net operating savings. If the estimate were only $100, the arithmetic would change materially.', [('Incremental initial cost','$1,500'),('Assumed annual net saving','$150'),('Simple payback: 1,500 / 150','10 years'),('At $100 annual saving','15 years')],
'These are invented inputs, not a forecast for a real system. An unverified incentive should not be subtracted from the amount the owner must initially fund.',
['Actual combination ratings / evidence / comparison scope','Usage / climate / utility inputs / estimate source','Incremental cost / annual net saving / exclusions','Sensitivity / financing / maintenance assumptions','Incentive verification / program date / funding status'],
['A label is not presented as a household savings promise.','Payback assumptions are reproducible.','No unverified incentive is counted as confirmed cash.'], 'Request a written comparison with assumptions before paying a premium for an efficiency claim.')

chapter('Review heat-pump and backup decisions','Match the equipment and operating plan to the property.',[
'A heat pump can provide heating and cooling, while the proposed configuration and supporting scope vary by property. ENERGY STAR buying information discusses ducted and ductless arrangements and climate-related selection questions [3]. Ask the qualified contractor which configuration suits the actual home.',
'Record the conditions used to assess heating performance and the proposed role of any backup system. Keep performance documents, selected models and professional explanations together. A marketing phrase such as cold climate is not a complete description of the selected installation or its operating plan.',
'Clarify any retained furnace, supplementary equipment, controls and utility changes in the complete scope. Ask how the proposed parts work together and who confirms the arrangement. The workbook does not establish a balance point, backup capacity or control sequence.',
'Compare the full installed cost and responsibilities rather than assuming that adding or retaining backup equipment is always necessary or always unnecessary. Keep any local incentive or operating-cost assumption separate until verified. The useful result is a documented property-specific decision and a clear support contact.'
], 'A fictional proposed heat-pump scope is $10,500. A separately assessed backup and control package adds $1,200. The example simply records an option; it does not recommend that package for any home.', [('Defined heat-pump scope','$10,500'),('Assessed optional package','$1,200'),('Combined scope if approved','$11,700'),('Actual performance suitability','Requires professional review')],
'The addition is valid only if backup and controls are not already in the base proposal. Record the design reasoning and complete utility scope before approval.',
['Configuration / served area / professional recommendation','Selected performance conditions / supporting documents','Backup role / retained components / control reviewer','Full installed additions / exclusions / utility changes','Verified assumptions / decision rationale / support'],
['Climate suitability is professionally assessed.','Backup is a documented decision, not a blanket rule.','Complete system scope is compared before price.'], 'Ask the contractor to explain the proposed heating plan using the actual model and property assessment.')

chapter('Write the complete installation scope','Specify the result and the interfaces around the equipment.',[
'Describe removal, supply, installation, connected work, protection, restoration and completion records. Give each task a code so it can be traced through quote revisions and changes. An equipment model list and a total price alone do not explain the complete service being purchased.',
'Record which components are retained and what assessment supports that decision. Identify the distribution, utility and control work, including anything supplied or performed by another party. Ask the contractor to reconcile interfaces rather than treating another trade\'s exclusion as an owner problem discovered on installation day.',
'State how unknown conditions and substitutions will be documented, priced and approved. A defined allowance is a placeholder for specified uncertain scope, not a general authorization to spend. Keep quantity, unit rate and inclusions visible where a conditional task will be priced that way.',
'Include permits and inspection coordination where applicable, commissioning documents, owner instruction and warranty responsibilities. Required design and technical acceptance remain with the qualified professionals and responsible authorities. This guide helps the owner request and retain the record; it does not certify an installation.'
], 'A fictional complete replacement line has $5,800 equipment, $2,100 installation, $400 removal and $300 agreed owner instruction and documentation. Supporting utility work is separately included elsewhere.', [('Equipment','$5,800'),('Installation','$2,100'),('Removal','$400'),('Instruction and documents','$300'),('Defined line total','$8,600')],
'If the installed package already includes removal or handover, do not add those tasks twice. Compare the entire project only after all separate scopes are reconciled.',
['Task codes / equipment / retained components','Remove / install / connect / restore inclusions','Other trades / exclusions / accountable parties','Unknown-condition and substitution approval process','Permits / commissioning / instruction / warranties'],
['Connected trade interfaces have one responsible owner.','Allowances have defined scope and evidence.','Completion deliverables are written into the proposal.'], 'Issue the same complete scope checklist to every bidder before selecting a final offer.')

chapter('Plan installation access and downtime','The project includes the route into the home and the handover.',[
'Ask the contractor to assess equipment access, delivery, working space and removal routes. Record any limitations involving doors, stairs, occupied rooms or external equipment positions. An assumed standard installation may not describe the property, and an access issue can change both price and schedule.',
'Agree the expected interruption period and a communication plan for occupants. The household may need temporary arrangements during harsh weather or while other trades complete supporting work. Use appropriately qualified advice for those arrangements; do not improvise equipment connections from this handbook.',
'Coordinate delivery, approvals, supporting trades and commissioning dates. A quoted installation day does not prove that every dependency is ready. Record the person who confirms the start conditions and the action required if a model or connected task is delayed.',
'Keep site protection, cleaning, restoration and disposal in the accepted scope. Record how unexpected conditions are communicated and who approves a price or date change. A practical access plan reduces ambiguity without making the homeowner responsible for the contractor\'s technical work or site procedures.'
], 'A fictional project includes $180 additional access handling and $220 agreed protection, both excluded from the equipment package. An expected two-day interruption has no separately priced household cost yet.', [('Access handling','$180'),('Protection','$220'),('Known logistics additions','$400'),('Temporary household arrangement','Open item; confirm cost')],
'An unresolved household cost should not silently become zero. Confirm actual needs, responsibility and pricing before treating the all-in plan as complete.',
['Delivery / access / removal routes / site assessment','Occupant interruption / contacts / temporary arrangements','Dependencies / start conditions / confirming person','Protection / cleaning / disposal / restoration scope','Delay and unknown-condition approval / open costs'],
['Actual access has been assessed.','Interruptions are communicated to occupants.','Open costs and dependencies remain visible.'], 'Request a short access, downtime and handover plan with the selected proposal.')

